PaulaNET driver disk
====================

This disk puts your Amiga on the network through the WiFi floppy
board's Wi-Fi, using the board as drive DF1.

PaulaNET and its driver, PaulaNET.device, are by Rob Smith
(RobSmithDev): https://github.com/RobSmithDev/PaulaNET
Copyright (c) 2026 RobSmithDev. All rights reserved.
The PaulaNET.device on this disk is webadf's build of his
driver (his source, commit 0afd5fa) with LZ4 compression
added: with board firmware 1.16.0, data the Amiga receives
travels compressed on every Amiga, and data it sends on a
68020 or faster. PaulaNET Config is his, unmodified. Both
are on this disk with his permission, under his terms; they
are not covered by webadf's MIT licence.

The Install script and this ReadMe are from webadf.

You need a TCP/IP stack, such as AmiTCP_NG or Roadshow, and no
other drive answering as DF1.

1. Double-click Install (or type: Execute PaulaNET:Install).
   It copies PaulaNET.device to DEVS:Networks and, unless one is
   already there, writes DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0.
2. In a Shell, type:  AddNetInterface eth0
3. To go online at every start, add that line to S:User-Startup.

Keep this disk in DF1 while you are online: the driver checks for
it when it starts. PaulaNET Config is not needed with the WiFi
floppy board; its Wi-Fi is set on the board's web page.

Transfer
========

Transfer moves files between the webadf web app and a folder on
your Amiga, both ways. (It was called Receive; Install removes
the old C:Receive.) Install copies it to SYS:Tools, with its
icon, and to C:.

Receiving: files you send from the web app -- from the Upload
page (Send to Amiga), from a disk's Browse page, or a whole disk
as an .adf file -- are saved into a folder you choose. Start it
by hand: double-click its icon, or type Transfer in a Shell. In
the window, choose the folder and press Save. The default folder
is RAM:. Close the window to stop. Files sent while Transfer is
not running wait on the server for 24 hours.

In a Shell:
  Transfer RECEIVE [TO <folder>] [SAVE] [GUI] [TRUST]
  Transfer SEND <files> [GUI] [TRUST]
  Transfer GUI
RECEIVE is the default: plain Transfer receives too. TO uses a
folder for this run, SAVE also stores it, GUI opens the window.
Ctrl-C stops it.

Sending (board firmware 1.19.0 or newer): drop icons or drawers
on its window, press Send..., or type  Transfer SEND DH0:file
in a Shell. They appear under From the Amiga on the web app's
Upload page. Transfer sends only to the board it trusts: the
first one it meets ("Trusted board AB12"; the web app shows the
same four digits). If another one connects, press Trust this
board (or use TRUST) to accept it. A file cut off part way
continues when you send it again within 24 hours.

Nothing is overwritten: a second Foo.lha arrives as Foo_2.lha,
and nothing is written outside the folder. While Transfer runs,
it listens on TCP port 6800, and any computer on your network
can drop a file into the folder -- but files go out only to
the trusted board.
